{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794815000000-engineer-d1-r18-package-continuation.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794810000000-engineer-d1-r17-reconstruction-package.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794805000000-architect-d1-executable-closure-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"# D1 r18 — observational dependency retention, package incomplete\n\nWorktree mirror; no commit. This is a partial continuation **below the requested items 1–2 checkpoint**. It is not an execution manifest, reconstruction package, D1 verification, or historical runtime attestation. No fixture was reconstructed under the inventory, and no existing store, outcome, admission coordinate, root, or event was changed. The row-lane-only and existing bounds restrictions remain.\n\n## What was captured\n\nAdded `src/cljc/yang/correspondence_inventory.py`, a standalone observational capture helper. It archives each entry of an explicitly supplied resolved classpath in order, and the supplied runtime directory, into individual SHA-256-addressed tar files. Directory archives include the contents present at capture time, including uncommitted/generated files, modes and symlinks; regular classpath files are archived as files. It records resolved absolute source paths as descriptive observations, kinds, archive sizes and hashes. Capture checks existing addressed objects; `verify` reopens and hashes every referenced archive. It does not extract archives, provide safe extraction, or establish a closed executable dependency set. Absolute and escaping symlinks can be present. Original top-level symlink spellings are resolved before capture.\n\nThe local snapshot was created from `clojure -Spath -M:test` and `/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/java/21.0.2`. The manifest is `target/d1-r18-inventory.json`, SHA-256 `60e51d6f350a32f8ce2d8f05dee3daff8eb191317a77b69ac97577d8fd78ea9a`; the objects are under `target/d1-r18-inventory-store/objects/<sha256>`. It contains 154 ordered classpath entries and one runtime archive, totaling 450,129,920 referenced archive bytes. The store also contains unreferenced objects from the preceding capture. In a separate Python process, all referenced sizes and hashes were verified after the capture process exited. These `target/` artifacts are scratch: no retention owner, durable publication, address discovery service, or availability guarantee has been established. The JSON hash is its identity for this observation, not a `dao.jing` manifest address or the ruled reconstruction profile.\n\nAn inventory scan found one absolute/escaping symlink in the captured archives: the runtime's `content/Contents/Home` points to `/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/java/21.0.2`. A portable runtime restoration must explicitly resolve this layout or select and retain a suitable distribution; blindly extracting or executing this archive is not a valid solution. Inventory paths and archive membership do not prove source/resource completeness, classpath/resource semantics, absence of ambient reads, or that the historical producer loaded these bytes. The package has not been bootstrapped from these objects.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n1. Resolve the runtime link and prove the restored layout on a supported platform; identify and retain the actual transitive source/resource and binary/native closure. Establish a durable retention owner and discovery mechanism and verify availability after process exit. A SHA-256 archive inventory is not yet integrated with the existing `dao.jing` byte-bundle/manifest draft.\n2. Construct the versioned execution manifest and reconstruction profile with the full build/load plan, explicit ordered classpath and resource resolution, working-directory mapping, JVM options, properties, environment, locale/encoding, initialization, pipeline stages and contracts. Reject undeclared inputs and unsupported targets. The inherited `yang.correspondence-manifest` remains a draft inventory validator with unresolved expansion; it is not an executable closure validator.\n3. Implement the standalone fresh-process reconstruction entrypoint, resolving compiler sources from the verified package rather than the checkout. Verify fixture/source/adaptation → program/options → compiled and stamped roots and all claimed metadata, source hash, binding, and correspondence; test refusal for missing/tampered objects and mismatches. The inherited producer-side replay still uses the checkout and does not meet this obligation.\n4. Substantiate the direct path's expander bypass against pinned composition and checked execution, or retain the applicable expansion context and events. Absence of events is insufficient. Historical expansion uncertainty remains.\n5. Evaluate the corpus as a **later reconstruction**, reusing only admission coordinates supported by retained facts; independently derive classification inputs from verified context sources. Complete the remaining publication, image/trace, bounded-run and independent verification obligations before any reliance claim. The Jupiter r9b store remains unavailable on this laptop.\n\nThe supplied earlier architect ruling filenames and continuation-4 are not present under those names here, as already noted in r17; the third ruling, available findings chain and declaration drafts were consulted. This inventory does not amend any ruling.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe Python tests were written first and initially failed for the missing helper. After implementation, `python3 test/yang/correspondence_inventory_test.py` passed 2 tests, covering ordered capture, file bytes and symlink retention, fresh object verification, corruption, missing input and nested-store refusal. A separate process verified the actual snapshot. `clojure -M:kondo --lint` on the inherited changed Clojure machinery and tests plus `latte_oracle_test.clj` reported 0 errors and 0 warnings. The frozen `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite, corpus reconstruction, tagged lane or commit was run.\n# D1 r17 — assembly linkage pilot, reconstruction package incomplete\n\nWorktree mirror; no commit. This is a **partial continuation below the requested steps 1–3 checkpoint**. No execution manifest, retained runtime/package, standalone reconstruction entrypoint, later corpus reconstruction, companion publication, or verification result was produced. Nothing here establishes D1 reliance. The existing stores and outcomes remain unchanged; the row-lane-only and bounds/execution restrictions remain in force.\n\nThe executable-closure adjudication `1794805000000-architect-d1-executable-closure-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.findings.md` is now present and was read. It explicitly permits a later reconstruction package while distinguishing reproducibility from historical runtime attestation; successful replay cannot invent missing admissions or expansion events. The earlier r17 note saying that adjudication and r14 were absent is superseded: both are now present. The two earlier architect ruling files and continuation-4 named in the handoff were not found under their supplied names here; the recorded r5 numeric-fidelity implementation is not a substitute for those original texts. The Jupiter r9b store is not present on this laptop.\n\n## Completed partial work\n\nThe inherited `yang.correspondence-text` docstring correctly labels its profile as **serialization metadata**, not an executable reconstruction profile. Its stored value is unchanged. The earlier r16 inventory's inference that original runtime capture must precede *any* later reconstruction is corrected by the new ruling; original-process provenance remains a separate, unresolved claim.\n\nIn `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`, `d1-assemble` now names the existing direct assembly of instrumented forms and call, library inclusion, and compiler options. `kernel-compile` uses that same composition. The producer-side `d1-check-reconstruction!` now rebuilds that program and options from the retained forms and call and compares them with the retained program/options using the existing order-, metadata- and numeric-fidelity relation before replay. A small test checks refusal of a changed call and changed options. This narrows the compilation/hash split in **that producer-side pilot only**. It shares the assembler and compiler loaded by the invoking checkout; it does not reconstruct fixture adaptation from verified context sources and does not establish independent verification or an executable closure. The separate `verify-retained-fixture!` remains unchanged and still compiles the retained program without this assembly check.\n\nThe prior local observation `target/d1-r17-classpath-inventory.json` describes the current `clojure -Spath -M:test` invocation: 154 ordered entries (144 files, 10 directories), with sizes and SHA-256 for regular files, and Java 21.0.2 on macOS aarch64. Its SHA-256 is `a6b29f8306f845829c409705a5e97e03541e05824d2b0882302eb2b8ba87ecb0`. It is a scratch observation, not retained dependency bytes or a manifest. It does not inventory directory contents or retain the runtime, identify all source/resource/native dependencies, or prove isolation. It is not evidence of the historical process.\n\n## Unresolved acceptance blockers\n\n1. The current text-profile equality check in `test/yang/correspondence_verification.clj` still selects the invoking checkout's compiler, not a verified manifest-driven fresh process. No manifest or executable reconstruction profile exists.\n2. The producer-side assembly check above is not the required independently reconstructed fixture/source/adaptation-to-root chain. The existing retained-fixture gate still has the original split; no corpus was evaluated under the new check.\n3. Constructor names in the existing independent gate still come from the producer snapshot; verified context-source inputs must instead derive them and be compared with the snapshot.\n4. The six retained roles do not publish an execution manifest or an expansion-applicability/context record. The observed direct composition merits investigation but absence of events alone is not a bypass proof; historical expansion remains uncertain.\n5. `yang.correspondence-dependency` remains a byte-bundle primitive and makes no completeness claim. The new manifest, classpath ordering, runtime layout/modes/links, load/launch plan, transitive availability, supported-host refusal and isolated entrypoint still need implementation and validation.\n\nThe later reconstruction must fetch addressed source/resource and binary bytes and an addressed runtime, verify them after process exit, run without the producer checkout or ambient dependency caches, and compare every claimed chain output. It must reuse only retained admission coordinates supported by the facts and preserve missing history as incomplete. Derive classification inputs, publish the side artifacts, verify image derivations and complete lane evidence under a finalized adequate declaration, and publish a separate verification result. No existing roots or events should be adjusted to make replay pass.\n\n## Validation\n\nThe new focused assembly test was run red first (missing `d1-assemble`), then passed: 1 test, 3 assertions. The existing producer-side close/reopen replay test passed: 1 test, 6 assertions. The single live compilation against frozen site evidence passed: 1 test, 12 assertions. Kondo on the changed oracle test reported 0 errors and 0 warnings; `git diff --check` passed. The frozen v1 `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite, tagged lanes, or corpus run was executed.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-17 09:58:43 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 16:58:43 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\nRULING: (other) — D1 requires an addressed, runnable reconstruction package containing exact source/resource and binary dependencies, a retained runtime distribution, and explicit execution instructions; generated classes may be derived, and historical process provenance remains a separate claim.\n\nMy earlier “executable dependency closure” requirement was underspecified. It did not define the retained artifacts, the bootstrap boundary, or the distinction between reproducing a compilation and proving which bytes a historical process executed. This amendment supplies those definitions.\n\nThe r16 escalation is correct. Its dispatch’s assertion that the retained corpus “holds the closure CONTENT” is contradicted by the inventory. The missing compiler/runtime cannot be extracted from the six retained roles.\n\nNone of the three proposed options is sufficient unchanged:\n\n- **(a)** unnecessarily requires preserving all compiled classes. A runtime image also does not, by itself, preserve compilation inputs, environment settings or expansion events.\n- **(b)** provides useful labels and instructions, but coordinates and version strings do not retain exact artifacts or ensure their future availability.\n- **(c)** removes a required relationship. Independently checking declarations and bindings against retained ASTs does not establish that those ASTs came from the retained source/adaptation inputs.\n\nD1 needs the source-to-root derivation checked. For this implementation, that means running the prescribed compilation and comparing its outputs, followed by independent correspondence verification. It does **not** require reconstructing the original JVM heap, JIT output or machine.\n\nThe following replaces §4.1’s executable-dependency clause.\n\n> **1. Meaning of reconstruction closure.**  \n> The reconstruction profile shall reference a closed, versioned execution manifest. Its closure consists of every retained artifact and explicit input needed to execute the declared reconstruction pipeline on a supported host, terminating at the declared platform/bootstrap boundary.\n>\n> A closure is complete only when reconstruction can start in a fresh process using the fetched package and declared inputs, without reading the producer’s checkout, dependency caches, previously loaded namespaces or undeclared resources.\n>\n> Git revisions, Maven coordinates, toolchain versions and download URLs are descriptive provenance or retrieval hints. They do not substitute for addressed, available artifact bytes.\n\nThe execution manifest must specify these components:\n\n| Component | Required retained content |\n|---|---|\n| Sources and resources | Exact files implementing source adaptation, assembly, compilation, stamping, canonicalization and other claimed transformations; their transitive source dependencies and resources. Include relevant uncommitted/generated files. A commit archive is sufficient only for files actually supplied by that archive. |\n| Binary dependencies | Exact resolved dependency jars, required precompiled classes and non-platform native libraries, with content identities. Preserve classpath order and resource-resolution rules. `deps.edn` alone is insufficient. |\n| Runtime | An addressed JDK/JRE distribution appropriate to the declared platform. Use a JDK where the build plan requires its compiler. Retain the distribution bytes or an addressed file manifest sufficient to restore its layout, modes and links. |\n| Build/load plan | Explicit source-loading or build steps, inputs, tool identities, arguments and expected products. Source-loaded Clojure classes and JIT products need not be persisted. An indispensable opaque precompiled artifact must be retained. |\n| Launch configuration | Entrypoint, ordered classpath/module path, JVM options, working-directory mapping, relevant properties, environment values, locale/encoding and initialization rules. Inputs affecting results must be explicit; undeclared ambient inputs are disallowed. |\n| Pipeline and contracts | Ordered stages, input/output roles, compiler arguments—including the existing `true` argument—grammar/encoding identities, text/fidelity profile, stamping rules, source-hash rule and coverage policy. |\n| Expansion context | The applicable expansion policy and, when expansion occurs, the retained context and event/attempt closure described below. |\n\n> **2. Concrete initial implementation.**  \n> Use a **source-loaded JVM reconstruction package**: retain the exact required repository/dependency source and resource bundles, resolved binary jars, runtime distribution and launch manifest. Load the compiler from those sources in a fresh process.\n>\n> Do not archive transient generated classes merely because the original process generated them. They are derived execution products. If the selected package instead loads AOT classes, those classes must be retained or generated by its declared build plan.\n>\n> Package and retain shared dependencies once; fixture recipes may reference the same addresses. Large artifacts may reside in a separate content-addressed archive, provided their addresses resolve through the declared retention/discovery mechanism. A publisher’s URL without retained availability does not satisfy this requirement.\n\n> **3. Platform and trust boundary.**  \n> The manifest declares supported OS/architecture and required platform facilities. The retained runtime and declared host facilities form an explicit execution trust boundary; this contract does not recursively demand rebuilding the operating system or hardware.\n>\n> Content verification proves artifact identity, not compiler correctness. Reconstruction proves that the selected interpreter reproduces the retained results. Independent checks establish the additional correspondence obligations.\n>\n> A package is portable as addressed data across process and host boundaries. Execution requires a supported target. A different target needs a separately identified runtime/package variant and the same exact-output gates; it may not silently substitute its installed Java.\n>\n> Refuse missing artifacts, unsupported targets, incompatible profiles, undeclared dependencies and output mismatches.\n\n> **4. Expansion applicability and history.**  \n> Distinguish deterministic frontend rewrites from event-producing expansion stages. Compiler routines such as control-form lowering are reproduced by executing the pinned compiler; they do not require invented `yin.vm.macro` events.\n>\n> For a pipeline that invokes the row-native expander, retain its input batch, initial relevant context/store closure, macro implementations, supplied origin/attempt coordinates, events and resulting correspondence. Capture through the event-returning interface. Replay must reproduce the required records and relationships.\n>\n> A pipeline that bypasses that stage shall declare it inapplicable and substantiate that declaration through its pinned composition and checked execution path. Absence of stored events is not evidence of bypass.\n>\n> A missing historical generating event cannot be recovered merely by observing an equal output root.\n\nThis distinction is supported by the current code: [Yang’s frontend description](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:16) explicitly separates frontend compilation from row-native macro lookup. [expand-batch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1722) returns its log and updated context; [expand](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1840) discards those from its return value. The current direct compile/stamp path supports investigating an inapplicable expansion stage; it does not establish every historical process’s behavior.\n\n> **5. Verification obligations.**  \n> Reconstruction shall establish the entire claimed chain:\n>\n> retained fixture/source/adaptation inputs → assembled program/options → compiled root → stamped root and transformation correspondence.\n>\n> Recompute the oracle source hash from its prescribed inputs. Check that those inputs actually generate the program being compiled. Reconstruct claimed metadata and provenance; independently derive site declarations and bindings. Producer snapshots are expected results or diagnostic evidence, not authoritative inputs to classifications that the verifier is required to derive.\n>\n> Existing image-derivation, trace-completeness and attribution requirements remain. A successful dependency fetch or root comparison alone is not D1 acceptance.\n\n> **6. Historical records and later reconstruction.**  \n> A package identifies the interpreter used for reconstruction. It does not, without separate evidence, attest that the original producer process loaded those exact bytes.\n>\n> A package assembled after an admission must be identified as a later reconstruction package. It may support verification of that admission’s retained inputs and outputs if the declared recipe implements the required interpretation, the complete derivation chain passes, and the required admission/expansion facts are available. It must not be described as an admission-time runtime capture.\n>\n> Missing historical environment evidence therefore does not automatically require discarding retained inputs. Missing required source, admission or generating-event information remains a blocker. Where that information cannot be recovered, perform a new admission with fresh coordinates and preserve the old record as incomplete.\n>\n> Never adjust expected roots, invent events or select ad hoc transformations merely to make a candidate package pass.\n\nThis last clause explicitly corrects the earlier ambiguity: **reconstruction is reproducibility of the declared interpretation; historical runtime attestation is a different claim.** The two must not be silently substituted for one another.\n\n> **7. Identity and retention.**  \n> Give the execution manifest and reconstruction profile immutable identities and explicit schemas. The recipe’s existing profile address may reference this manifest and the separate text-serialization profile; no D1 envelope or oracle-evidence schema change is required solely for this addition.\n>\n> Name the retention owner, retrieval mechanism and supported targets before acceptance. Verify transitive availability after process exit. Unknown manifests refuse reconstruction.\n\nThis follows “derive, don’t persist”: generated classes can be rebuilt; opaque dependency artifacts and explicit inputs must remain available. It does not imply a multi-GB per-fixture image. Actual shared-package size remains unmeasured.\n\nThe implementation sequence is:\n\n1. **Correct the r16 inventory premise.** Preserve the existing store and outcomes. Classify its text profile as serialization metadata, not an executable reconstruction profile.\n2. **Build one explicit reconstruction package.** Inventory the resolved classpath and required sources/resources, retain their bytes and the runtime distribution, and specify source loading and launch configuration. Reuse the r15 byte-bundle helper where appropriate.\n3. **Add a standalone reconstruction entrypoint.** Fetch and verify the package, start a fresh process, reconstruct assembly and compilation, and compare all claimed outputs. Do not resolve compiler code from the invoking checkout.\n4. **Resolve expansion applicability.** Record a substantiated bypass for the direct pipeline, or capture the actual expander context and logs. Keep historical uncertainty visible.\n5. **Evaluate the existing corpus under the new package.** Record it as later reconstruction. Reuse retained admission coordinates only where the required admission facts exist; do not infer missing history from replay success.\n6. **Finish independent verification and publication.** Derive classification inputs, publish the required side artifacts, verify image derivations and complete lane evidence, and issue a separate verification result. Existing bounds and execution restrictions remain.\n\nStatic review found these defects or acceptance-blocking gaps:\n\n| Location | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| [correspondence_verification.clj:148](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/correspondence_verification.clj:148), [line 159](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/correspondence_verification.clj:159) | Equality with the current text profile checks serialization/runtime labels, then replay calls the already-loaded checkout compiler. It neither selects nor verifies an executable closure. Replace this dependency with the manifest-driven reconstruction entrypoint. |\n| [correspondence_verification.clj:156](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/correspondence_verification.clj:156), [correspondence_verifier.clj:312](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:312) | Program compilation and `forms`/`call` hashing are checked separately. The gate does not establish that the hashed adaptation generates the compiled program. Reconstruct and check the assembly implemented at [latte_oracle_test.clj:2524](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:2524). |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:4494](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:4494), [correspondence_verifier.clj:317](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:317) | Constructor names are supplied by the snapshot and used to derive site classifications. They affect ambient classification at [line 78](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/correspondence_verifier.clj:78). Derive them from retained, verified context-source inputs and compare with the snapshot instead of trusting that set. |\n| [latte_oracle_test.clj:4497](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:4497) | The six-role publication contains no execution manifest or expansion-applicability/context record. Its successful storage/reopen cannot establish either. |\n| [correspondence_dependency.clj:18](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/src/cljc/yang/correspondence_dependency.clj:18) | This is correctly a byte-bundle primitive. It lacks executable roles, classpath, launch/build semantics and completeness validation. Those belong in the new manifest layer. Its current disclaimer is accurate; it is not a defective closure validator because it does not claim to be one. |\n\nThe existing partial gates correctly disclaim complete D1 verification. Preserve those disclaimers until the new reconstruction and independent-verification obligations pass.\n\nStatic review only. No suites run and no files edited.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r{2,3,4,5,6,7,8}*.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794790000000-engineer-d1-r14-companion-subset-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794795000000-engineer-d1-r15-upstream-construction.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md collab/yang-clojure/1794390804000-cc3-bounded-run-declaration-v2.md > /tmp/d1-r19-context.txt\nwc -l /tmp/d1-r19-context.txt\ncat src/cljc/yang/correspondence_inventory.py test/yang/correspondence_inventory_test.py'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r{2,3,4,5,6,7,8}*.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794790000000-engineer-d1-r14-companion-subset-retention.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794795000000-engineer-d1-r15-upstream-construction.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md collab/yang-clojure/1794390804000-cc3-bounded-run-declaration-v2.md > /tmp/d1-r19-context.txt\nwc -l /tmp/d1-r19-context.txt\ncat src/cljc/yang/correspondence_inventory.py test/yang/correspondence_inventory_test.py'","aggregated_output":"     832 /tmp/d1-r19-context.txt\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Capture an observational classpath and JDK inventory, not a D1 package.\n\nArchives retain directory entries, modes and symlinks. They are SHA-256\naddressed; a verified archive still does not prove dependency closure or that\nexternal symlink targets and ambient inputs are available.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport tarfile\nimport tempfile\n\n\ndef digest(path):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as stream:\n        for chunk in iter(lambda: stream.read(1024 * 1024), b\"\"):\n            h.update(chunk)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef capture(paths, runtime, store):\n    paths = [Path(p).resolve(strict=True) for p in paths]\n    runtime = Path(runtime).resolve(strict=True)\n    store = Path(store).resolve()\n    if not paths or not runtime.is_dir():\n        raise ValueError(\"classpath and runtime directory required\")\n    for path in [*paths, runtime]:\n        if not (path.is_dir() or path.is_file()):\n            raise ValueError(\"unsupported entry\")\n        if path.is_dir() and (store == path or path in store.parents):\n            raise ValueError(\"store must be outside captured directories\")\n    objects = store / \"objects\"\n    objects.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    def archive(path):\n        fd, name = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=objects, prefix=\".partial-\")\n        os.close(fd)\n        temporary = Path(name)\n        try:\n            with tarfile.open(temporary, \"w\") as tar:\n                tar.add(path, arcname=\"content\", recursive=True)\n            sha = digest(temporary)\n            size = temporary.stat().st_size\n            destination = objects / sha\n            if destination.exists():\n                if digest(destination) != sha:\n                    raise ValueError(\"existing object corrupt\")\n                temporary.unlink()\n            else:\n                os.replace(temporary, destination)\n            return {\"original-path\": str(path),\n                    \"kind\": \"directory\" if path.is_dir() else \"file\",\n                    \"sha256\": sha, \"size\": size}\n        finally:\n            temporary.unlink(missing_ok=True)\n\n    return {\"schema\": \"d1-observational-inventory-v1\",\n            \"claim\": \"inventory only; closure and execution unverified\",\n            \"classpath\": [archive(p) for p in paths],\n            \"runtime\": archive(runtime)}\n\n\ndef verify(manifest, store):\n    if (manifest.get(\"schema\") != \"d1-observational-inventory-v1\"\n            or not manifest.get(\"classpath\")):\n        raise ValueError(\"unsupported inventory\")\n    for entry in [*manifest[\"classpath\"], manifest[\"runtime\"]]:\n        sha = entry[\"sha256\"]\n        if (len(sha) != 64 or any(c not in \"0123456789abcdef\" for c in sha)):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid address\")\n        path = Path(store) / \"objects\" / sha\n        if not path.is_file() or path.stat().st_size != entry[\"size\"] or digest(path) != sha:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing or corrupt object: \" + sha)\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--classpath-file\", required=True)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--runtime\", required=True)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--store\", required=True)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--manifest\", required=True)\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    classpath = Path(args.classpath_file).read_text().strip().split(os.pathsep)\n    if any(not path for path in classpath):\n        raise ValueError(\"empty classpath entry\")\n    manifest = capture(classpath, args.runtime, args.store)\n    verify(manifest, args.store)\n    Path(args.manifest).write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\nimport importlib.util\nimport tarfile\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\nMODULE = Path(__file__).parents[2] / \"src/cljc/yang/correspondence_inventory.py\"\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"inventory\", MODULE)\ninventory = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(inventory)\n\n\nclass InventoryTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_capture_reopen_and_detect_corruption(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n            base = Path(tmp)\n            source = base / \"source\"\n            source.mkdir()\n            (source / \"uncommitted.clj\").write_text(\"hello\")\n            (source / \"link\").symlink_to(\"uncommitted.clj\")\n            jar = base / \"dependency.jar\"\n            jar.write_bytes(b\"jar bytes\")\n            runtime = base / \"jdk\"\n            runtime.mkdir()\n            (runtime / \"java\").write_bytes(b\"runtime bytes\")\n            store = base / \"store\"\n            manifest = inventory.capture([source, jar], runtime, store)\n            inventory.verify(manifest, store)\n            self.assertEqual([\"directory\", \"file\"],\n                             [entry[\"kind\"] for entry in manifest[\"classpath\"]])\n            self.assertEqual(2, len(manifest[\"classpath\"]))\n            obj = store / \"objects\" / manifest[\"classpath\"][0][\"sha256\"]\n            with tarfile.open(obj) as archive:\n                self.assertEqual(b\"hello\", archive.extractfile(\"content/uncommitted.clj\").read())\n                self.assertEqual(\"uncommitted.clj\", archive.getmember(\"content/link\").linkname)\n            obj.write_bytes(b\"corrupt\")\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n                inventory.verify(manifest, store)\n\n    def test_missing_and_nested_store_refuse(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n            base = Path(tmp)\n            with self.assertRaises(FileNotFoundError):\n                inventory.capture([base / \"missing\"], base, base / \"store\")\n            with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\n                inventory.capture([base], base, base / \"store\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
